00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:09.650
And after that, I work in the--

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.720
I work as an analyst.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.640
I work in the
analytical industry.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.620
So I started as an
analyst and then

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.839
transitioned to data
scientist, and then

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:20.839
manager of data science,
senior manager of data science,

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:24.010
and eventually VP
of data science now.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.460
And a bit, probably a little
bit fun fact for myself,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.220
because when I see Rachael's
self-introduction, and Rachael

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:31.990
said, that I play guitar.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.770
I said, OK, this is
just me, I like guitar.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.390
I like rock and roll a lot.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:40.810
So I play guitar like an
immature, but I like it a lot.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.490
So maybe I started--

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.740
it was-- I started
in maybe 2000.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:48.790
I was a high school in China.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:52.600
And then I saw-- happened to
saw famous video of "Smell

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.440
Like Teen Spirits," by
Nirvana on Chinese television.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:01.670
And this song resonates with
my personality and my character

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:02.860
so well.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:06.040
I started from then, I
maybe spend four or five

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.760
years listening to rock roll
crazily, I would say that.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.040
Maybe I would spend
five, six hours

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.890
everyday just listen to
all this type of music.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:22.810
Maybe you remember, iPod used
to have 160 gigabyte iPod.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.420
It can hold more
than 40,000 songs.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:28.662
And this is just a product
for people like me.

00:05:28.662 --> 00:05:29.840
[LAUGHS]

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.420
So I spent a lot of time
on rock and roll music.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.100
I listened to the music.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:42.910
I wrote music reviews, album
reviews for some online forums,

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:46.360
I even published it in
some music magazines.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.040
I started for four
or five years.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.280
And when I came to
the United States,

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.060
so after I graduated
with my master's degree,

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.190
I got to entry level
job as an analyst.

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:01.135
And during that time it's
for me it's kind of like a--

00:06:01.135 --> 00:06:03.880
I would call it, I have
two phases in my life.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.190
So during the daytime,
I work as an analyst.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:10.030
I was very analytical,
very rational, very nerdy.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:13.560
But probably after
work, after 8:00 PM,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:17.330
I got changed, put on my
all my punk rock gear.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.720
And then, I don't know whether
other people-- by the way,

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:20.828
I'm from Dallas, Texas.

00:06:20.828 --> 00:06:22.870
I don't know whether other
people in the audience

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:24.340
from Dallas, Texas too.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.710
In Dallas, there is a
famous district called

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.820
Ti Pao in the downtown area.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:33.080
There are a lot of music clubs,
pop, rock and roll shows,

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.300
all the concerts held up there.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.390
So I spent a lot
of nights there.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.690
So probably especially
every weekend.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:43.840
So after 8:00 PM I
just go-- went there.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:45.640
I joined the concert.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:48.400
I hang out with a lot of
rock and roll bands there.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.950
From there I meet a lot of my--

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:55.750
a lot of bands I idolized, like
[NON-ENGLISH],, Sonic Eagles,

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.200
all these bands.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.570
So I just hung out with them.

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:00.970
And then probably until 1:00 AM.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.250
And the next day I
just go back to work.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.458
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I love that.

00:07:04.458 --> 00:07:07.870
And then, such life
continued for five years

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.030
until maybe 2015.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.290
There was a milestone.

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:13.150
I got married.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.470
So after that, I
since change a lot.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:19.960
So instead of go to having
my regular nightlife

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.570
like every day or every
other day, probably

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.330
I can't go to a concert
show maybe other bands.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:26.750
That's bad.

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.677
I remember the first
show I tried to brought

00:07:29.677 --> 00:07:30.760
my wife to the first show.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.720
It was a show by the
WHO and Junkyards

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.970
at the American Airline Center.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:38.620
And after that
she told me, oh, I

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.650
can't believe that you listen
to that kind of noisy music.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:44.140
[LAUGHS] So I kind
of decide, probably I

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.870
need to stop going to
a concert that often.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:50.290
And then, so since then,
I can only go to a concert

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:51.580
once a month.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.670
And then in 2017,
another thing happened.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:58.690
So my wife was pregnant
with our first son, William.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:00.760
So I remember, I
can still remember,

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:04.870
my last concert I
went was in May 2017.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:05.860
That's the last one.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:09.520
And then William was
born in January 2018.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.180
And then, so it's just
very busy, just crazy.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.000
The first year was just crazy.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:19.190
And then in 2020, William
was almost two years old.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:21.070
I thought, he is old enough.

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:22.960
He can sleep at
night by himself.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:26.840
Probably I can go back to my
music life a little bit more.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.060
And then, you know what
happened, the pandemic.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:30.760
So everything closed.

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:34.059
All this rock and roll clubs,
the famous, the Tourist,

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:37.789
Club Dada, Pub Factory,
everything was closed.

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:40.989
So I had to, of course, I
had to put the plan on hold.

00:08:40.989 --> 00:08:43.270
And sadly, right now,
a lot of the clubs,

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:47.170
they never reopen again
due to this shock.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:47.670
And then--

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:49.900
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: So, I have
to ask you, do you ever

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:54.248
mix music and data science now?

00:08:54.248 --> 00:08:54.790
Absolutely.

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:56.332
Yeah, I can finish
that very quickly.

00:08:56.332 --> 00:08:59.090
So after that, for me,
data science is a science.

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:01.370
But I also want to talk
to people in my team.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:04.720
So if you want, you really do
it-- want to do it very well,

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.810
treat it like an art,
not just science.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:10.300
In order to be a very
good data scientist,

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:12.700
I believe we all
need to be creative.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:15.970
And I believe the creative
all need to possess

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:18.220
some artistic creativity.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.380
And for me, for
myself, the creativity

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:21.400
comes from the music.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:25.480
I love it.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:29.530
I absolutely love live music
and finding live music.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:32.520
So now I know who to ask
for recommendations whenever

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.918
I go to Texas then.

00:09:34.918 --> 00:09:35.418
Sure.

00:09:35.418 --> 00:09:38.370
So we can't put an ending to
talk about my music life here.

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.050
So probably, we can start
to talk about data science

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:41.820
or my experience as an analyst.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.195
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Yes, absolutely.

00:09:43.195 --> 00:09:45.936
Hey, we're good with making it
a rock and roll hangout too.

00:09:45.936 --> 00:09:49.590
But Yu, I'm really curious,
while we wait for questions

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:53.760
to come in from everybody, what
made you so interested in data

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.280
science from the beginning?

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:58.390
Oh, I just resonate
with my nature.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:00.880
So I'm an analytical person.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.760
I'm more focused on details.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:07.140
And I like to find
a patterns from data

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.570
and then use it to
make predictions.

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:13.410
And I like to work with
people from the academia,

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.910
like from universities, or from
the research team of big tech

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:18.730
companies.

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:22.930
So everything fits perfect
as a data scientist.

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:27.350
So that's why I started my
job as a data scientist.

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:31.160
What has the progression
been like for you,

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:33.560
moving from an analyst,
to data scientist,

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:37.640
to leading a data science team?

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.310
The first thing that because
when I work as an analyst,

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.870
most of my job is
ad hoc analysis.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.260
So I pull data, I
build the reports,

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:48.080
I do some other analysis,
and present my results

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.500
to the leadership team.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:52.310
However, I later found out,
so instead of just doing

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:57.760
something ad hoc, I want to
do something more systematic.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:01.580
Again, I'm always--
because my second degree

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:04.500
was the operations research
as basically mass modeling.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.090
So I still want to
build models for all

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:09.500
the other analytical jobs.

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:13.890
And because my first job was
for a healthcare company.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.770
So at that time I
wanted to see, so can we

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:19.430
use all the data to predict
the behavior of patients?

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:22.580
And I started-- I built
my first predictive model

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:23.510
during that time.

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:27.120
And after that, I feel that
this is what I want to do.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.190
So that's why I
want to pivot to--

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:32.990
and it's a little-- it's a small
pivoting from analyst to a data

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:34.010
scientist.

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:37.130
And then after that,
because I changed to--

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:39.120
I switched to a financial
service company.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.480
So our company doing
subprime loans,

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:43.490
and which is
subprime auto lender.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:46.190
Because if you want to buy a
car, you'll go to a dealership.

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:48.050
And if you can not
pay cash, you'll

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:50.570
need to apply for a car loan.

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:54.290
And because if your credit
score is not that well,

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:57.590
for our definition, subprime
means that people with credit

00:11:57.590 --> 00:11:59.690
score less than 620.

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:02.000
And then for that
customer segment,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.920
there are a lot of risk there.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:06.230
So you need to build a
lot of predictive models

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:09.710
of predictive rates at that
price to manage the price,

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.540
manage how we
servicing the loan,

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:12.980
how we originate the loan.

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:18.080
So this is why our entire
subprime lending business

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.150
is driven by data science.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:24.200
And because of this, I feel
that I have my knowledge

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:28.310
and my detail, my interest,
plays a super important role

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:29.280
for the business.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.210
So that's why this is
the industry I like.

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.400
And then I started
as data scientist.

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:38.090
And gradually I feel, it's
not just about modeling.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:39.710
So you can just
sit there building

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.000
model coding every day.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:43.470
But this is the most basic work.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:45.090
So if you want to
do it really well,

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:47.674
you need to understand
the business part.

00:12:47.674 --> 00:12:51.510
You need to understand, so what
problem the business is facing?

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.410
And how can you formulate
a business problem

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.000
into a data science problem?

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:00.950
And then, build a model
is also the simplest part.

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:03.790
And then, with this,
and then I tried

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.880
to started to spend
my time to collaborate

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:09.760
with all the other
teams in the company,

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:15.490
try to understand their world,
how is the sales team working?

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:16.990
How is the marketing
team working?

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:19.510
How is the origination
teams working?

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:22.720
And but gradually, because
I have more understanding

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:27.700
of the business, I gradually
move up in the career

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:29.440
hierarchy, I'll call it.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:33.040
And after I become senior
manager of data science,

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:37.280
I feel my data science
knowledge is not enough.

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:40.690
So if you go to a higher
level, it's not about coding.

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:42.670
It's about not
about data science.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:46.060
Suddenly, you'll need some
hard skills from other areas.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.050
For example, you need to
understand accounting.

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:49.342
You need to understand finance.

00:13:49.342 --> 00:13:50.800
You need to
understand marketing.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:55.420
And for all of these, I need
to learn something probably.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:58.280
Of course, ideally you can
also outsource these skills.

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:01.233
Because if you are a leader,
you need some accounting,

00:14:01.233 --> 00:14:01.900
hire accountant.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:03.317
You need some
financial knowledge,

00:14:03.317 --> 00:14:05.260
hire financial manager
or financial analyst.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:08.150
And if you need marketing,
hire a marketing manager.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:09.880
These hard skills,
you can outsource.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:12.310
But they are also another
part of, probably,

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:13.540
I call them soft skills.

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:15.430
You can only do it by yourself.

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:19.570
For example, how to
build a productive team.

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:22.660
How to, for example, how to
do business negotiations.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:26.307
All these skills you cannot
outsource them to other people,

00:14:26.307 --> 00:14:27.640
let other people help you do it.

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:28.820
You need to do it yourself.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:31.720
And that's why I decided I
need to go back to school.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:33.580
I need to pursue
an EMBA program.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:36.460
Probably this is the
best time for me.

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:39.400
And I started program last
year at Cornell University.

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:40.900
And later, if you
are interested,

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:45.832
I can explain why I think
that this program feeds--

00:14:45.832 --> 00:14:47.290
a lot of people in
the audience who

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:51.410
are aware because of its
format and because of its goal.

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:56.505
And so, but right now,
Rachael, I go back to you,

00:14:56.505 --> 00:14:58.150
see if you have any question.

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:00.410
Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:02.350
And I know we
shared in the chat,

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:05.230
if you want to ask questions
anonymously, you can use Slido.

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:07.000
But feel free to just
raise your hand here

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.790
if you want to jump in
the conversation too.

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:13.750
But Yu, I love the
way that you explain

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:15.700
working with different
parts of the business

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:19.100
to actually understand
the business problems.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:23.290
And I was wondering what that
process looks like for you

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:26.260
of building relationships
with different people

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:27.400
across the business.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:30.490
Is it like joining
some of their meetings?

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:32.110
How do you go about that?

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:35.045
Actually, it all
started with networking.

00:15:35.045 --> 00:15:36.670
Because networking,
I know a lot of it,

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:39.520
because I work with very
analytical, I would say,

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:40.820
a very nerdy team.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.990
So a lot of the people there
are, I just want to code.

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:47.120
I want to build a
fantastic model.

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:50.920
I do not want to spend too much
time to network with people

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:53.020
from other business lines.

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:56.860
But actually, this is
probably, I would say,

00:15:56.860 --> 00:15:58.180
it's not the right attitude.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:01.312
Because if you want to
collaborate with other people,

00:16:01.312 --> 00:16:02.770
first you need to
get to know them.

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:07.010
You need to understand how
their businesses-- and yeah,

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:08.950
another word, probably
most, is actually you

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:10.390
need to become their friends.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:13.420
So usually I reach out
to them and say, do

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:14.530
a self-introduction.

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:16.040
And this is what I do.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:17.980
This is what my team can do.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:19.150
So let's go out for lunch.

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:21.250
Let's go for-- let's
grab a cup of coffee.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:22.750
Let's talk for 15 minutes.

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:24.345
Let's see what
kind of business--

00:16:24.345 --> 00:16:25.720
what kind of
difficulties you are

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:27.640
having now in your business.

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:29.470
If you are a sales
team, are you worrying

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:31.960
about how to increase
the volume of sales?

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:34.570
If you are a loan
originations team,

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:37.180
are you worried about how to
increase the capture rate?

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:38.630
And then, let's talk about that.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:41.980
Then I will see, how can
I connect my data sets

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:45.160
knowledge with the problem
you are facing now?

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:49.060
See whether I can build
a model to help you

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:50.770
solve that problem you have.

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:54.350
Usually, the collaboration
starts with this.

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:57.370
And when you build
a model for them,

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:59.875
how do you share that with them?

00:16:59.875 --> 00:17:01.090
What does that look like?

00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:03.212
Well, I would say
that probably I think

00:17:03.212 --> 00:17:04.420
that's a very important part.

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:07.630
Because data scientist has to
be a wonderful storyteller.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:11.619
It's easy to build a model
today because we have AutoML,

00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:14.937
we have very automated
pipeline that basically we

00:17:14.937 --> 00:17:15.770
can't have it later.

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:17.980
So we design our
model on the pipeline,

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:20.109
or like a streamline
assembly line.

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:23.530
So basically, even if you have
no knowledge of data science,

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:25.630
you just change some
hyper-parameters,

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:28.180
and they'll click a button,
of course, in RStudio.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:30.970
And then, after two
hours or two days,

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:33.070
depends on the size
of your data set,

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:35.920
you can get a bunch of models.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:39.410
And a lot of them, mostly
this model performs well.

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:40.873
But this is only the beginning.

00:17:40.873 --> 00:17:42.540
And then, the difficult
part is that you

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.620
need to sell these models,
like your own product.

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:49.390
You need to let the business
owners, stakeholders, buy

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:50.260
this model.

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:53.800
You can build a very
good model, a risk.

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:57.340
However, you need to be able
to let your dealer sales

00:17:57.340 --> 00:17:59.350
managers understand this model.

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:02.170
And they have to be-- they
should be able to explain this

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:03.670
to the dealers.

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:06.910
I cannot reach out to
our dealers, I build--

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:11.350
this is a model I built
measuring your risk.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:12.310
They won't buy it.

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.720
Dealers are, no, we don't
want to hear about it at all.

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:19.240
So the dealer-- so the model
needs to be transparent,

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:23.980
need to be explainable,
and to be that,

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:28.120
to do that, usually, my approach
is starts from the easiest one.

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:31.570
So usually, if we build
a new model, for example,

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:34.360
either to help
for the sales team

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:36.790
or measure the risk
of dealers, usually we

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:38.990
start with a simple
logistic regression model.

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:43.000
Because, frankly, everybody
understands a little bit

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:45.413
about logistic regression,
or pretends that they

00:18:45.413 --> 00:18:46.705
understand logistic regression.

00:18:46.705 --> 00:18:47.800
[LAUGHS]

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:50.860
So usually, if we present a
logistical regression model,

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:52.498
there won't be a
lot of pushback.

00:18:52.498 --> 00:18:54.040
They will say, OK,
I understand that.

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:55.923
Just tell me about
all the variables.

00:18:55.923 --> 00:18:58.090
And then, if the direction
the variables make sense,

00:18:58.090 --> 00:18:58.870
we'll buy it.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:02.230
And we can explain that to
the dealers, to the end users.

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:08.320
And after that, when you build
some relationship or trust

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:12.700
with your customers or
the user of these models,

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:15.940
then later you can gradually
push things forward.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:20.140
I would say, so next time we
can have some machine learning

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:21.010
algorithms.

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:24.400
The models we build using these
algorithms perform better.

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:25.840
They are more predictive.

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:27.310
And they are explainable.

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:29.530
We can try to show--
try to show that,

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:31.150
show you how to explain that.

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:34.270
And they are 100%
compliant because we are

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:36.170
in highly regulated industry.

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:38.140
So everything we
build, we are already

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:42.110
audited by the people from the
government annually every year.

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:44.080
We say, they will check
all the models we'll

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:46.510
build, to see whether
this is discriminatory,

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:50.380
whether all the variables
we use are compliant.

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:53.050
And we are sure that the
model is fully compliant,

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:57.710
is fully transparent,
and is accepted by--

00:19:57.710 --> 00:19:59.620
I won't say accepted
by the entire industry,

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:02.860
but it's accepted by a lot
of players in the industry.

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:04.480
So because we are
not the biggest

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:05.500
company in the industry.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:08.470
We are probably the third
or fourth-largest subprime

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:09.280
lenders.

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:11.770
So our company,
well, our leadership

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:14.600
is a little bit conservative--
their culture is.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:17.470
So what we always
say, we have a model,

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.330
this is the algorithm
we just figure out.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:21.490
They will try to say,
are we the first one

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:22.840
using this in the industry?

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:24.430
If the answer is
yes, they probably

00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:26.560
say, oh, probably
we should wait.

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:29.830
And if we said, probably Capital
One is using this right now.

00:20:29.830 --> 00:20:32.100
Or maybe Santander, maybe--

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:33.100
is using this right now.

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:34.780
They will say, OK,
then let's go ahead.

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:36.580
[LAUGHS]

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:39.880
I see there's a lot of
love for logistic regression

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:41.080
in the chat there.

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:43.750
[LAUGHS] But Alan, I see
you just asked A question.

00:20:43.750 --> 00:20:46.700
Do you want to jump in?

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:47.200
Sure.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:48.370
Yeah.

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:49.270
Hi.

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:53.500
I'm really curious about how
your organization has treated

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:56.560
your shift in roles from
individual contributor

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:58.270
to a VP of data science?

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:01.570
And curious if-- what's
that meant for you in terms

00:21:01.570 --> 00:21:04.810
of figuring out what is
my mission at each stage,

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:07.060
and whether you had to
create that for yourself

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:10.810
as you became VP, or if that was
already a well established role

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:14.200
that you could
slide into and know

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:15.880
what success looks like there.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:17.500
How did you make
those transitions?

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:20.530
You talked a little bit about
leadership and about the

00:21:20.530 --> 00:21:21.730
learning the business stuff.

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:24.130
But I'm wondering, did
the organization help you,

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:27.580
or were you on your own
to go, oh, I'm a VP now.

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.963
What does that mean?

00:21:29.963 --> 00:21:31.130
Thank you very much, Alan.

00:21:31.130 --> 00:21:32.650
First, very good question.

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:35.090
And to explain
that, first of all,

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:38.290
I will say that my
leadership of the company,

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:42.460
they are super supportive for
my growth for my transition.

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:46.190
And they provide a lot
of help, either help

00:21:46.190 --> 00:21:48.400
from the perspective
of leadership

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:52.570
to help me to get exposure
to senior leadership

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:56.590
and help me learn a lot of
things on how to lead a team,

00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:59.830
how to work with other
teams, and they even

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:03.220
provide some financial
help for my EMBA study.

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:05.590
And on the other
side, our company

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:08.690
is not a very big company.

00:22:08.690 --> 00:22:11.680
So a lot of the time,
you need to-- and so,

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:14.080
I'm the first VP of,
I would say, yeah,

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:17.013
I'm the first person to hold
the title VP of data science

00:22:17.013 --> 00:22:17.900
in the company.

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:22.280
So basically, I need to
build a lot of procedures,

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.880
processes all by myself.

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:27.550
It's kind of now I have a
team of data scientists.

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:30.770
And I need to define
my own function.

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:32.890
So now you are VP
of data science now.

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:35.380
So it doesn't make sense
you're still sitting there

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:36.640
building models every day.

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:37.890
You need to do something else.

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:41.800
Because you should dedicate
these jobs to your reports.

00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:42.310
Yes.

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:45.550
And then, I figure
out, the goal I

00:22:45.550 --> 00:22:47.670
made for myself is
that as a leader,

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:50.800
as a VP of data science, the
most important thing for me

00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:52.700
is to grow my own team.

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:56.680
So I have some data
scientists that report to me.

00:22:56.680 --> 00:23:00.670
My goal is that I want to let
them know that so one day you

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:02.650
will be in my shoes.

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:03.970
I won't be there forever.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:05.876
And one day you will
move up, up, up,

00:23:05.876 --> 00:23:07.570
and then you will be here.

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:10.330
And then, in order
to be prepared,

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:14.920
I want you to start to
either advise your own self,

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:16.870
you need to make
clear career goals,

00:23:16.870 --> 00:23:20.320
what you want to achieve in the
next three years, five years,

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:22.690
not just from a technical
standpoint, no stress.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:26.680
OK, I want to master how
to build posting models,

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:30.987
how to propose some innovations
to our current model,

00:23:30.987 --> 00:23:32.320
your own process, not just that.

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:36.160
I want them to make
goals on their behaviors.

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:41.980
Like how to, I mean, how to
develop their own leadership

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:46.240
skills, how to engage, how
to create opportunities

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:48.640
for themselves to be a leader.

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:52.910
And I will be 100%
supportive for their growth.

00:23:52.910 --> 00:23:55.180
I believe this, right
now, this is my goal.

00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:59.150
So for me, probably I
will say that maybe--

