00:48:24.560 --> 00:48:25.060
Cool.

00:48:25.060 --> 00:48:28.570
Thank you.

00:48:28.570 --> 00:48:31.690
One anonymous question
that's over on Slido

00:48:31.690 --> 00:48:35.530
is, what is the biggest
challenge or project

00:48:35.530 --> 00:48:38.320
that you and your team are
trying to tackle right now?

00:48:41.480 --> 00:48:47.473
If you say biggest
challenge, well, building--

00:48:47.473 --> 00:48:50.140
traditionally, we are building a
lot of machine learning models.

00:48:50.140 --> 00:48:51.540
So I won't say any of--

00:48:51.540 --> 00:48:53.910
some of them are more
difficult than others.

00:48:53.910 --> 00:48:57.180
But I won't say that
any specific ones

00:48:57.180 --> 00:48:59.070
are especially challenging.

00:48:59.070 --> 00:49:00.630
One thing we are
doing right now is

00:49:00.630 --> 00:49:04.560
we are trying to build
some optimization models.

00:49:04.560 --> 00:49:06.942
My major is operations research.

00:49:06.942 --> 00:49:09.150
So it's not predict model,
it's the machine learning.

00:49:09.150 --> 00:49:12.130
For example, some of
traveling salesman,

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:15.060
I guess, I'm sure some
people just know that.

00:49:15.060 --> 00:49:18.090
And this is new
field for my team.

00:49:18.090 --> 00:49:22.150
And it's I won't say
it's challenging,

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:23.530
but it's very interesting.

00:49:23.530 --> 00:49:26.838
So for example, we want
to-- we have our sales team.

00:49:26.838 --> 00:49:28.380
They need to visit
different dealers.

00:49:28.380 --> 00:49:30.588
So it's a kind of classic
traveling salesman problem.

00:49:30.588 --> 00:49:34.570
That we have these dealers,
we know their location.

00:49:34.570 --> 00:49:36.690
So basically, we
have the distance

00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:39.330
between every two points.

00:49:39.330 --> 00:49:42.030
And then we know the
starting point, probably

00:49:42.030 --> 00:49:45.090
the home base of the
salesman, or sales manager.

00:49:45.090 --> 00:49:47.100
And then every
day, a manager only

00:49:47.100 --> 00:49:49.980
travel a certain amount,
a certain number of miles,

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:52.140
can only visit a certain
number of dealers.

00:49:52.140 --> 00:49:54.940
And then, for each dealer,
we have our expectations.

00:49:54.940 --> 00:49:57.540
So probably a potential benefit.

00:49:57.540 --> 00:49:58.260
A visit.

00:49:58.260 --> 00:50:02.880
And then we need to build an
optimization model to say,

00:50:02.880 --> 00:50:07.890
finally, given this, tell
dealer sales manager what?

00:50:07.890 --> 00:50:10.020
On Monday, this is
the list of dealers

00:50:10.020 --> 00:50:11.790
we think you should visit.

00:50:11.790 --> 00:50:14.370
And we also give them miles.

00:50:14.370 --> 00:50:16.790
You can start from dealer
A, and after A, you

00:50:16.790 --> 00:50:19.830
travel to dealers C,
and then this comes in.

00:50:19.830 --> 00:50:22.530
This is the project we
are working right now.

00:50:22.530 --> 00:50:24.600
The challenging part
is the implementation.

00:50:24.600 --> 00:50:27.780
And because it can be very
complicated, because a dealer

00:50:27.780 --> 00:50:30.780
sales manager, they may
have different home base.

00:50:30.780 --> 00:50:35.130
And maybe the area or the
territory he or she manages

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:36.600
is so big.

00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:40.170
And then, you need to
add some other concerns,

00:50:40.170 --> 00:50:43.890
like how much time he can travel
in the morning, how much time

00:50:43.890 --> 00:50:45.270
he can travel in the afternoon.

00:50:45.270 --> 00:50:48.750
If you add all the
constraints, the problem

00:50:48.750 --> 00:50:51.070
can get more complicated.

00:50:51.070 --> 00:50:53.590
So right now this is the
problem we are trying to tackle.

00:50:53.590 --> 00:50:57.000
Also, it's a challenging one.

00:50:57.000 --> 00:50:58.540
Thank you.

00:50:58.540 --> 00:51:02.040
Another question over on
Slido is, is your group

00:51:02.040 --> 00:51:04.170
considered a revenue generator?

00:51:04.170 --> 00:51:06.450
Have you or your team
developed something which

00:51:06.450 --> 00:51:09.850
made higher-ups take notice?

00:51:09.850 --> 00:51:10.360
Yeah.

00:51:10.360 --> 00:51:11.360
I would say, absolutely.

00:51:11.360 --> 00:51:14.050
Because actually, I
asked the same question

00:51:14.050 --> 00:51:16.570
while I take the accounting
class in the EMBA program.

00:51:16.570 --> 00:51:20.650
I said, so our
program, we shall we

00:51:20.650 --> 00:51:22.540
be considered as revenue
generator or not?

00:51:22.540 --> 00:51:24.790
[LAUGHS] And the
professor said, yes,

00:51:24.790 --> 00:51:26.800
because you generate
a lot of, I mean,

00:51:26.800 --> 00:51:29.530
in intelligent--
intelligent assets.

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:32.140
So and especially for
the financial services,

00:51:32.140 --> 00:51:34.540
for the lending
industry, data science--

00:51:34.540 --> 00:51:35.770
it's driven by data science.

00:51:35.770 --> 00:51:40.040
Because you'll have
a lot of money here.

00:51:40.040 --> 00:51:43.388
And a lot of people will
reach out to you to say,

00:51:43.388 --> 00:51:44.680
could you borrow me some money?

00:51:44.680 --> 00:51:46.720
I want to finance my vehicle.

00:51:46.720 --> 00:51:50.590
And if you cannot use your data
science to mitigate the risk

00:51:50.590 --> 00:51:55.540
well, to make the rejection,
to make the approval or decline

00:51:55.540 --> 00:51:59.170
decision wisely, then you
will lose all your hand very

00:51:59.170 --> 00:52:00.960
quickly.

00:52:00.960 --> 00:52:03.510
So because of this,
I believe that is,

00:52:03.510 --> 00:52:08.820
again, definitely data science
is core part of the revenue

00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:10.380
generator of our business.

00:52:14.390 --> 00:52:16.520
Thank you.

00:52:16.520 --> 00:52:20.180
I am not sure how to
ask this question.

00:52:20.180 --> 00:52:23.720
But so I just watched
recently The Big Short.

00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:25.430
And so, I'm just--

00:52:25.430 --> 00:52:27.470
I'm curious if there
are certain things

00:52:27.470 --> 00:52:30.350
that you have to do
as a data science team

00:52:30.350 --> 00:52:37.270
to make sure that
certain areas aren't

00:52:37.270 --> 00:52:43.310
lending to too many people who
might not be able to pay back.

00:52:43.310 --> 00:52:49.263
Looking at individual areas
as compared to others?

00:52:49.263 --> 00:52:50.680
Could you rephrase
the question?

00:52:50.680 --> 00:52:53.470
So lending too many people?

00:52:53.470 --> 00:52:58.210
Well, I guess I'm, yeah,
Libby is helping me out there.

00:52:58.210 --> 00:53:02.360
Managing the risk
with subprime loans,

00:53:02.360 --> 00:53:04.880
do you have to look at
individual locations

00:53:04.880 --> 00:53:06.635
versus others, or
individual dealers?

00:53:06.635 --> 00:53:07.920
OK, gotcha.

00:53:07.920 --> 00:53:08.420
Of course.

00:53:08.420 --> 00:53:08.750
Yes.

00:53:08.750 --> 00:53:10.167
So let me explain
it a little bit.

00:53:10.167 --> 00:53:12.110
It's very interesting,
the subprime industry.

00:53:12.110 --> 00:53:14.450
So basically, the
principal business, that we

00:53:14.450 --> 00:53:15.510
have a lot of money.

00:53:15.510 --> 00:53:18.230
We are trying to lend them out.

00:53:18.230 --> 00:53:21.110
We will start from the
safest customer segment.

00:53:21.110 --> 00:53:23.143
And they will keep
lending, lending, lending,

00:53:23.143 --> 00:53:24.560
and then the risk
of the portfolio

00:53:24.560 --> 00:53:26.000
will increase,
increase, increase,

00:53:26.000 --> 00:53:30.050
until it reaches the
threshold of reach to a level,

00:53:30.050 --> 00:53:31.430
the risk is too high.

00:53:31.430 --> 00:53:32.920
We'll stop.

00:53:32.920 --> 00:53:36.310
And then, again, in
this whole process,

00:53:36.310 --> 00:53:38.868
the risk is just measured
by a lot of machine

00:53:38.868 --> 00:53:39.910
learning models we build.

00:53:39.910 --> 00:53:42.500
For example, we receive an
application from a customer

00:53:42.500 --> 00:53:45.800
say, I need to borrow
$10,000 to buy a car.

00:53:45.800 --> 00:53:48.460
The first thing we need to do is
whether we approve or decline.

00:53:48.460 --> 00:53:52.120
And then we use bureau data,
we use some alternative data,

00:53:52.120 --> 00:53:54.940
like payday loans, these kind
of things, to build a model,

00:53:54.940 --> 00:53:58.160
to make a decision, maybe
in 10 or 15 seconds.

00:53:58.160 --> 00:53:59.860
And then say, OK,
if we approve it,

00:53:59.860 --> 00:54:01.120
how decline this application.

00:54:01.120 --> 00:54:04.372
If approved, then there are
some other decisions to make.

00:54:04.372 --> 00:54:05.830
So if approve it,
what's the price?

00:54:05.830 --> 00:54:07.480
What's the APR we should charge?

00:54:07.480 --> 00:54:09.130
What kind of deal
we can provide?

00:54:09.130 --> 00:54:13.840
The customer will said, I want
to borrow $40,000 for a BMW.

00:54:13.840 --> 00:54:16.360
But we built some models
where it says, sorry,

00:54:16.360 --> 00:54:18.310
we can only approve--
we can only give you

00:54:18.310 --> 00:54:20.560
maybe $15,000 for a Toyota.

00:54:20.560 --> 00:54:22.790
This is determined by the model.

00:54:22.790 --> 00:54:26.950
And after that, there are
also another similar series

00:54:26.950 --> 00:54:30.655
of models like probably
if we booked a deal, if we

00:54:30.655 --> 00:54:32.240
booked a loan, I will say that.

00:54:32.240 --> 00:54:34.810
So if we have the
loan in the portfolio,

00:54:34.810 --> 00:54:36.700
we'll be motivated,
well, so what's

00:54:36.700 --> 00:54:40.667
the default risk of this loan
during the entire life cycle?

00:54:40.667 --> 00:54:43.000
So we call the post-funding
models, something like that.

00:54:43.000 --> 00:54:45.800
And then we need to
have models manage it.

00:54:45.800 --> 00:54:47.710
So if the loan--

00:54:47.710 --> 00:54:49.240
how shall we service this loan?

00:54:49.240 --> 00:54:52.510
So if the customer is
a very risky customer,

00:54:52.510 --> 00:54:56.680
we shall call them every
month to say that you

00:54:56.680 --> 00:54:57.880
need to make the payment.

00:54:57.880 --> 00:55:00.070
Or is it our core
customer, we can just

00:55:00.070 --> 00:55:03.670
let him or her manage
the payment herself--

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:05.690
by himself or herself.

00:55:05.690 --> 00:55:08.770
So either way, there are a
bunch of, I would say, credit,

00:55:08.770 --> 00:55:11.710
we call scorecards, a
bunch of credit risk models

00:55:11.710 --> 00:55:15.850
behind the loan origination
and loan servicing

00:55:15.850 --> 00:55:17.180
the subprime industry.

00:55:17.180 --> 00:55:20.080
And this is what my team do.

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:21.910
And this is the
product of my team.

00:55:21.910 --> 00:55:24.400
And this is super interesting
because as a subprime,

00:55:24.400 --> 00:55:25.390
the risks are high.

00:55:25.390 --> 00:55:28.660
If for people, a lot of people,
most people in this audience,

00:55:28.660 --> 00:55:31.120
if we belong to the
prime or super prime,

00:55:31.120 --> 00:55:33.700
if the credit
score is very high,

00:55:33.700 --> 00:55:35.290
you do not need these models.

00:55:35.290 --> 00:55:38.380
You just need an Excel table.

00:55:38.380 --> 00:55:40.600
They just need to pull
your bureau score.

00:55:40.600 --> 00:55:43.960
And then based on table, say
this guy is in this interval,

00:55:43.960 --> 00:55:46.090
this is the interest
rate we will charge.

00:55:46.090 --> 00:55:46.720
That's it.

00:55:46.720 --> 00:55:49.070
Sorry.

00:55:49.070 --> 00:55:50.400
Thank you.

00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:53.270
I see two more
questions that I think

00:55:53.270 --> 00:55:55.640
we could cover before
the top of the hour.

00:55:55.640 --> 00:55:57.952
But Emilio, I see, you
just asked a question.

00:55:57.952 --> 00:55:58.910
Do you want to jump in?

00:56:03.210 --> 00:56:05.310
And I can read it if--

00:56:05.310 --> 00:56:05.810
OK.

